        "title": "1. Present and aorist active subjunctives",
        "practiceTopic": "subjunctive-active",
        "body": [
          "Mood tells us how a clause presents its action. The subjunctive commonly marks an action envisaged, proposed, or dependent on a purpose or other condition. It is not simply a future tense. In regular active forms, the characteristic vowels are ω and η: λύω, λύῃς, λύῃ, λύωμεν, λύητε, λύωσι(ν). The first singular looks like the indicative, so use context.",
          "The present subjunctive uses the present stem; the first aorist uses the sigmatic aorist stem without an augment: λύσω, λύσῃς, λύσῃ, λύσωμεν, λύσητε, λύσωσι(ν). A second aorist uses its own stem, as λάβω. The present presents an activity as ongoing or repeated; the aorist views it as a whole. Neither stem by itself means present versus past time here."
        ],
