    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Present and aorist active subjunctives: Mood tells us how a clause presents its action. The subjunctive commonly marks an action envisaged, proposed, or dependent on a purpose or other condition. It is not simply a future tense. In regular active forms, the characteristic vowels are ω and η: λύω, λύῃς, λύῃ, λύωμεν, λύητε, λύωσι(ν). The first singular looks like the indicative, so use context.",
        "2. Middle, passive, and contracted forms: The present middle/passive subjunctive has λύωμαι, λύῃ, λύηται, λυώμεθα, λύησθε, λύωνται. The first aorist middle has λύσωμαι, λύσῃ, λύσηται, λυσώμεθα, λύσησθε, λύσωνται. Middle voice is interpreted from the verb and context; it does not always mean an action done to oneself.",
        "3. Deliberation, exhortation, and prohibition: A deliberative subjunctive asks what one should do: τί ποιήσω; means “What should I do?” rather than a request for a fact already established. First-person plural exhortations invite a shared action: ἀκούσωμεν, “Let us listen.” The subject is included in the proposal.",
        "4. Purpose and clause reading: A purpose clause explains what an action aims to achieve. In primary sequence, ἵνα, ὅπως, or ὡς can introduce a subjunctive: ἐρωτῶ ἵνα μάθω, “I ask so that I may learn.” The intended result need not actually happen. Negative purpose normally has μή after the purpose conjunction."
      ]
    }
