    "title": "Who owns the better-fitting coat?",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-37-culture-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: A separate Persian textile and clothing scene, with garments and weaving tools; no duplicate of the judgment scene.",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction; details are interpretive, not a record of an exact ancient scene."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Cyropaedia",
        "body": [
          "The coat dispute introduces the work’s larger problem: how does someone learn to govern fairly? Read the childhood episode as a test of judgment that may later matter on an imperial scale. Mandane’s questions expose the difference between confidence and understanding. The useful arrangement of possessions does not itself establish a right to take them.",
          "Its eight books follow Cyrus the Great from education to conquest, imperial organization, and death. This Cyrus is the sixth-century founder of Persian power, not Cyrus the Younger of the Anabasis. The opening question is striking: why do people so often resist rulers, and how could Cyrus secure obedience across so many peoples? Childhood training becomes the starting point for a much larger inquiry into power.",
          "Xenophon combines campaigns, speeches, friendships, rivalries, and domestic stories to explore leadership. Rewards and persuasion stand beside military force; admiration for the ruler sits beside questions about dependence on him. The final account of decline after Cyrus complicates an uncomplicated success story. Read this as a crafted political and educational narrative, not a reliable transcript of sixth-century Persian court life.",
          "Reading invitation: compare the opening discussion of obedience in Book 1 with Araspas’s confidence about desire in Book 5 and its consequences in Book 6. Panthea’s story makes the problem personal: can someone entrusted with power govern himself? Then glance at the close of Book 8. What can a leader build that will survive him? The whole-work link opens Henry Graham Dakyns’s English translation, revised by F. M. Stawell."
        ],
        "links": [
          {
            "title": "Read the complete Cyropaedia — English translation",
            "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2085/2085-h/2085-h.htm"
          },
          {
            "title": "Wikipedia: Cyropaedia",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyropaedia"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A judgment with two different questions",
        "body": [
          "Imagine seeing only the boys and the sizes of their tunics. Giving each the garment that fits seems sensible. Now add the fact that the larger boy took the smaller boy’s property without consent. A useful allocation and a rightful transfer are no longer the same question. Cyrus’ teacher corrects the question he has answered, not merely his ability to notice size. The story lets a beginner’s mistake become a lesson about the limits of benevolent power.",
          "A tunic also represents labor and resources. Cloth must be produced, maintained, and replaced. The Culture/History banner suggests that material world, but its workshop details are a modern reconstruction. Neither the illustration nor the literary story establishes the precise appearance of a Persian school or garment exchange."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Mandane and the education of a ruler",
        "body": [
          "Mandane questions Cyrus about what he has learned and about the difference between Persian and Median rule. She is an active participant in the educational argument, rather than a decorative family connection. Yet her voice is presented by a male Greek author writing a shaped account of Cyrus the Great. Keep the literary role and the limits of the evidence together.",
          "Timeline: this story concerns Cyrus the Great in the sixth century BCE. The Cyrus whom Xenophon accompanied in 401 BCE was Cyrus the Younger, a different person. A shared name does not turn the Cyropaedia into a memoir of the Anabasis expedition."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Try the judgment twice",
        "body": [
          "First allocate the two coats by fit and explain your reason. Then make a second judgment after learning who owned each coat and whether the exchange was voluntary. Write two sentences explaining what changed. You may propose a voluntary exchange, but identify it as your solution rather than an event recorded in the passage.",
          "Translation workshop: translate paragraphs 1–2 with glosses; bracket the subjunctives in paragraphs 3–4; then translate paragraph 5 continuously. In your final version distinguish the source-based episode from the course’s questions about power. What evidence would you still need before declaring an exchange fair?"
        ]
      }
    ],
