        "1. δίδωμι and τίθημι in the subjunctive: Athematic verbs do not keep their indicative endings in the subjunctive. The present of δίδωμι retains reduplication: διδῶ, διδῷς, διδῷ, διδῶμεν, διδῶτε, διδῶσι(ν). Its aorist uses the shorter stem: δῶ, δῷς, δῷ, δῶμεν, δῶτε, δῶσι(ν). Do not infer aorist simply from a circumflex.",
        "2. ἵστημι and ἵημι: The present subjunctive of ἵστημι is ἱστῶ, ἱστῇς, ἱστῇ, ἱστῶμεν, ἱστῆτε, ἱστῶσι(ν). The intransitive second aorist is στῶ, στῇς, στῇ, στῶμεν, στῆτε, στῶσι(ν), “take a stand.” The transitive first aorist στήσω means “set something up” in the appropriate subjunctive clause; distinguish it by construction from future indicative στήσω.",
        "3. Middle forms and principal parts: Middle subjunctives retain middle endings. δίδωμι has present διδῶμαι and aorist δῶμαι; τίθημι has τιθῶμαι and θῶμαι. The aorist middle θῶμαι can mean “establish for myself” or “adopt,” depending on the object. Voice is a relationship between subject and action, not a fixed English reflexive.",
        "4. Proposals, purposes, and commands: Use the same clause questions as in Lesson 37 with the less familiar stems. τί δῶμεν; deliberates; δῶμεν τὰ δῶρα exhorts; δότε τὰ δῶρα commands a plural audience. δῶμεν is not an infinitive, and δότε is not a subjunctive."
