    "translation": "The soldiers, saved from many dangers, had not escaped all their troubles. They still disputed about pay, food, and the generals’ actions. In the assembly some accused Xenophon of striking men. He did not leave, but wished to give an account. He therefore asks who the accuser is and when he suffered what he alleges.\n\nOne of the men stands up and says that he was struck by the general. Xenophon asks why. For it is not enough, he says, to say that you suffered something; one must also make clear what preceded the act. Those present listen to both. The charge is serious, but the response does not immediately resolve every difficulty. One must therefore examine carefully what is said.\n\nXenophon reminds the soldiers of the march. In winter many were exhausted, and the enemy were nearby. Anyone remaining behind was in danger. The general therefore fears that the men may perish; he is afraid that they may be unwilling to get up. He said that he ordered them to march for their safety, not for his own gain.\n\nWhenever a soldier is exhausted, he needs help. Whoever abandons his companion harms not only him but also the community. The general says these things so that the listeners may examine the need. Yet not every action is just because its doer considers it useful. Let us therefore investigate both the manner and the cause.\n\nIn the narrative the soldiers remember their former hardships, and Xenophon defends his own intention. We ask two questions: what does the accuser say, and what does the general reply? Then we ask who writes the account. For the man being judged and the writer are the same. This neither makes everything false nor proves everything true.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 5.8.1–12 and selected 5.8.13–26. Independently composed course adaptation, not a verbatim quotation. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1170/1170-h/1170-h.htm",
