    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "fear-clauses",
        "title": "1. Fear clauses: μή and μή οὐ",
        "practiceTopic": "fear-clauses",
        "body": [
          "After a verb of fearing, μή introduces the feared event: φοβοῦμαι μὴ ἀπόληται, “I fear that he may perish.” It is not translated “I fear that he may not perish.” To fear that something will not happen, use μή οὐ: φοβοῦμαι μὴ οὐ ἔλθῃ, “I fear that he may not come.” Separate the clause introducer from the negation.",
          "Primary sequence normally takes the subjunctive for a prospective event. After a historical main verb, an optative may appear, or the subjunctive may be retained. We support the optative in reading here and teach it fully in Lessons 42–43. Fear about a fact already treated as actual can use an indicative; do not force every fear into an imagined future."
        ],
