      {
        "id": "general-clauses",
        "title": "2. Indefinite relatives and general time clauses",
        "practiceTopic": "general-clauses",
        "body": [
          "An indefinite relative clause can use ἄν with a subjunctive: ὃς ἂν βοηθῇ, “whoever helps.” A general temporal clause uses combinations such as ὅταν (ὅτε + ἄν), “whenever,” and ἐπειδάν, “whenever/when.” The construction envisages any occasion or person meeting the description, rather than identifying one completed event.",
          "Relative pronouns still take their case from their role inside the clause. In ᾧ ἂν βοηθῇς, the dative is required by βοηθέω. Distinguish a definite temporal ὅτε with indicative from a general ὅταν with subjunctive. Past general conditions with optative will be treated systematically later."
        ],
