        "id": "indirect-questions",
        "title": "4. Reporting questions without losing the question",
        "practiceTopic": "indirect-questions",
        "body": [
          "An indirect question preserves what is being asked: ἐρωτᾷ τίς ἔπαισεν, “He asks who struck.” A question word such as τίς, πῶς, or πότε does not become a relative merely because the question is embedded. A yes/no question can use εἰ, “whether.”",
          "Track person and reference when shifting from direct speech. “Why did you strike me?” becomes “He asks why the general struck him.” The original “you” and “me” cannot both remain unchanged in a narrator’s report. At this stage use primary reporting frames; historical sequence is supported until formal instruction in Lesson 43."
