        "body": [
          "Normally a relative agrees with its antecedent in gender and number but takes case from its own clause. In attraction, a relative that would otherwise be accusative may adopt a genitive or dative antecedent’s case: τῶν ἀγαθῶν ὧν ἔχει, “of the good things which he has.” A relative can also have an understood antecedent: ὃ λέγει, “what he says.” Recover the clause’s function before parsing an attracted form.",
          "Prepositional prefixes change spelling at boundaries. ἐκ becomes ἐξ before a vowel; συν- can become συμ- before a labial and συγ- before a velar; final vowels can elide, as ἀπό + ἄγω → ἀπάγω. A rough breathing can affect the final consonant, as κατά + ἵστημι → καθίστημι. Use these patterns to recover dictionary entries, not to invent a meaning from the prefix alone."
        ],
