    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Infinitive reports and relative time: After verbs such as φημί and νομίζω, an assertion can be reported with an infinitive. A different expressed subject normally stands in the accusative: φησὶ τὸν ἄνδρα λέγειν. If the subject is the same as the reporting verb, it is often omitted and predicates may remain nominative: φησὶ δίκαιος εἶναι, “He says that he himself is just.”",
        "2. Perception participles and finite reports: Verbs of perception or knowledge often take a supplementary participle agreeing with the person or thing perceived: ὁρῶ τὸν δῆμον ὀργιζόμενον, “I see the people becoming angry.” The participle is part of the complement, not merely an optional circumstantial addition. Its case agrees with the relevant noun.",
        "3. φημί and negation: Learn present φημί, φῄς, φησί(ν), φαμέν, φατέ, φασί(ν), and imperfect ἔφην, ἔφησθα, ἔφη, ἔφαμεν, ἔφατε, ἔφασαν. Most present indicative forms are enclitic in their usual use; φῄς is accented. Position and neighboring accents can affect how enclitics are written in a sentence.",
        "4. The articular infinitive: An article can turn an infinitive phrase into a noun-like unit: τὸ χωρὶς ἕκαστον κρίνειν, “trying each person separately.” The article is neuter singular; its case follows the phrase’s function. The infinitive itself does not acquire case endings.",
        "5. Relative antecedents, attraction, and prefixes: Normally a relative agrees with its antecedent in gender and number but takes case from its own clause. In attraction, a relative that would otherwise be accusative may adopt a genitive or dative antecedent’s case: τῶν ἀγαθῶν ὧν ἔχει, “of the good things which he has.” A relative can also have an understood antecedent: ὃ λέγει, “what he says.” Recover the clause’s function before parsing an attracted form."
      ]
    }
  },
  "culture": {
    "title": "Victory, grief, and a hurried decision",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-40-culture-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: A separate view of the Pnyx and a labeled civic-process illustration, clearly marked as reconstruction.",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction; details are interpretive, not a record of an exact ancient scene."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Hellenica",
        "body": [
          "Our selection moves back to 406 BCE, before Athens’ defeat, to the aftermath of Arginusae. Victory and a failed rescue produce competing reports and a dispute about procedure. This is historical narrative shaped through speakers and decisions. Identify where the generals explain themselves and where the narrator describes the assembly’s response; their claims are not interchangeable.",
          "The seven books cover Greek affairs from 411 to 362 BCE, beginning where Thucydides’ unfinished history breaks off. They follow Athens’ defeat in the Peloponnesian War, oligarchy and democratic restoration, Spartan ascendancy, shifting alliances, and the rise of Thebes. The closing battle at Mantinea leaves uncertainty rather than a simple declaration that one city has permanently won.",
          "Xenophon organizes political and military events through commanders, assemblies, negotiations, speeches, and judgments about conduct. His attention is selective, and his experience and sympathies shape the narrative. Ask what a speech tries to accomplish, whose actions receive explanation, and what a compressed transition leaves unclear. A historian’s ordering of events can be as significant as an explicit verdict.",
          "Reading invitation: start with Book 2, chapter 3, on the Thirty, then follow chapter 4 through resistance and restoration. Compare the settlement with the later Eleusis episode instead of stopping at the hopeful return to Athens. Our module’s question has grown from control of appetite to restraint in civic life: what practices let former enemies inhabit one city? Read the complete work as a sequence of difficult choices, not a storehouse of uncomplicated moral examples.",
          "The procedural dispute asks how a democratic assembly should exercise its power when anger is intense."
        ],
        "links": [
          {
            "title": "Read the complete Hellenica — English translation",
            "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm"
          },
          {
            "title": "Wikipedia: Hellenica",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellenica"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Arginusae, 406 BCE",
        "body": [
          "Athens won the naval battle at Arginusae, but failure to recover men from disabled ships and the sea became the subject of fierce accusation. The generals’ explanation involved a storm and delegated rescue duties. Xenophon reports competing claims and an emotionally charged political response. A military victory and responsibility for losses could be debated at the same time.",
          "Do not confuse the council, the assembly, and an ordinary law court. The episode concerns an extraordinary collective proceeding against generals, not a template for every Athenian trial. The sequence of proposals and objections matters because procedure determines whose case can be heard separately."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Socrates and the disputed vote",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon reports resistance among the presiding officials to putting an unlawful proposal to a vote, followed by most giving way under pressure. Socrates refuses to act contrary to law. His objection is a concrete example of the tension between majority desire and procedural restraint.",
          "The objection did not prevent the outcome. Six generals who returned were condemned and executed; Xenophon later describes regret and attempts to pursue those held responsible for misleading the people. Avoid a reassuring ending in which a principled refusal alone saves everyone."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Build the sequence",
        "body": [
          "Draw five boxes: victory, failed rescue and explanations, accusation, procedural dispute, outcome. Under each write who speaks and what the account establishes. Place Socrates’ refusal in the fourth box, not in place of the fifth. Explain why evaluating the rescue and evaluating the trial procedure are related but distinct tasks.",
          "Translate one report in each form: infinitive, supplementary participle, and finite ὅτι clause. Then translate the final two paragraphs continuously. In your English, preserve the generals’ attribution and do not turn their explanation into an unqualified narrator’s fact."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "When was Arginusae?",
        "answer": "406 BCE."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why separate council, assembly, and ordinary court?",
        "answer": "The episode’s extraordinary procedure is not every Athenian trial."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What did Socrates’ refusal accomplish in the narrated outcome?",
        "answer": "It preserved his objection but did not prevent executions."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why distinguish rescue responsibility from trial procedure?",
        "answer": "One question does not settle the other."
      }
    ],
