    "translation": "Simonides discusses rule with Hiero. Hiero is a tyrant, Simonides a poet. Yet they speak not only about song but about the ruler’s life. Hiero has many possessions, but he is afraid and does not consider everyone nearby trustworthy. Having more does not necessarily provide a more pleasant life.\n\nSimonides points out another path. Let the ruler make not only his own household wealthier but the city better. Whenever the citizens prosper, the ruler receives greater honor. For public works benefit more people than private possessions do. Not only the greatest amount of silver but also goodwill provides power.\n\nTake care, then, that the citizens will work and practice their crafts. Give prizes to those doing well, so that others too may be willing to labor. Farmers will improve the land, while others will adorn public works. For the prize is often smaller than the expense that competitors willingly incur. In this way ambition leads toward the common advantage.\n\nSee that the guards will protect the citizens, not only you. Fear is not the same as friendship. Whenever people see power as beneficial to themselves, they fear it less. The best ruler does not compete with a private individual in wealth, but with other cities over the well-being of his citizens.\n\nSimonides gives this advice. The reader asks who benefits from the proposals. The farmer receives honor, the city more works, and the ruler a safer rule. But who judges the ruler? Who helps the person wronged? We must examine these questions before treating benefaction as identical with justice. For an increase in possessions does not resolve every question about what is just.\n\nLet us therefore read the argument as advice, not as testimony that everything happened in this way. The author supplies two voices so that we may compare being feared and being loved. The ruler’s well-being is related to the city’s well-being; but the measure of his authority still requires examination.\n",
