        "title": "4. Care and effort: ὅπως with future indicative",
        "practiceTopic": "care-object-clauses",
        "body": [
          "Verbs of care and effort can take an object clause with ὅπως and future indicative: ἐπιμελοῦ ὅπως οἱ φύλακες φυλάξουσιν, “Take care that the guards will protect.” The clause states the result one is to see to. A negative object clause uses ὅπως μή. The future is a finite indicative, not a subjunctive merely because ὅπως appears.",
          "Compare a purpose clause: ἆθλα δίδωσιν ἵνα οἱ πολῖται πονῶσιν, “He gives prizes so that the citizens may work.” First identify the governing verb and clause function, then the form. A ruler’s intention, a plan requiring effort, and an achieved result remain three different claims."
