    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Regular and irregular comparison: Regular comparatives commonly use -τερος and superlatives -τατος: σοφός, σοφώτερος, σοφώτατος. Learn the whole adjective and accent. Some frequent comparisons use other stems: ἀγαθός can give ἀμείνων/ἄριστος or βελτίων/βέλτιστος; μέγας gives μείζων/μέγιστος, and πολύς gives πλείων/πλεῖστος. Their shades of meaning depend on context.",
        "2. Comparatives in -ων and -ον: Comparatives such as μείζων have masculine/feminine nominative singular -ων and neuter -ον. The n-stem produces genitive μείζονος, dative μείζονι, and accusative masculine/feminine μείζονα. Neuter nominative and accusative agree: μεῖζον. Learn the accent as part of the form.",
        "3. Standards of comparison and adverbs: A comparative can take a genitive standard: μείζων τῆς οἰκίας, “larger than the house.” With ἤ, keep the compared items parallel in construction: σοφώτερος ἢ ὁ ἀδελφός. Distinguish the comparative’s standard from a possessive genitive elsewhere in the sentence.",
        "4. Care and effort: ὅπως with future indicative: Verbs of care and effort can take an object clause with ὅπως and future indicative: ἐπιμελοῦ ὅπως οἱ φύλακες φυλάξουσιν, “Take care that the guards will protect.” The clause states the result one is to see to. A negative object clause uses ὅπως μή. The future is a finite indicative, not a subjunctive merely because ὅπως appears."
      ]
    }
