    "title": "Written law and unwritten obligation",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-42-culture-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: A separate close view of a reconstructed inscribed law and a civic oath setting; no fabricated readable ancient inscription.",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction; details are interpretive, not a record of an exact ancient scene."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Memorabilia",
        "body": [
          "Book 4, chapter 4 puts a definition under pressure. Hippias asks Socrates to state a position about justice, and the discussion moves from conduct to law and unwritten obligations. The lesson asks you to reconstruct the argument fairly before judging it. A defense of Socrates can preserve a challenging objection as well as a confident response.",
          "Think of the four books as a gallery of encounters rather than a continuous biography. Book 1 answers accusations against Socrates and presents his religious practice and daily conduct. Books 2–3 explore family, friendship, useful work, and leadership. Book 4 gives sustained attention to education, including the questioning of Euthydemus. The movement from an ordinary habit to a public responsibility is characteristic of the collection.",
          "Xenophon writes as an advocate for a teacher condemned at Athens in 399 BCE. His Socrates improves other people through example, questions, and practical reasoning. That organizing purpose makes the work valuable evidence for an ancient interpretation of Socrates; it does not make every conversation a stenographic record. Notice who asks the questions, who changes position, and where the narrator supplies the judgment.",
          "Reading invitation: open Book 1, chapter 5, then Book 4, chapter 5. Ask whether mastery of appetite is merely a rule about pleasure or a condition for learning and dependable action. Keep a small dossier of the different kinds of evidence Xenophon offers: reported speech, observed habit, analogy, and the narrator’s praise.",
          "Track Hippias’s objections as carefully as Socrates’ answers, and identify what each successive example contributes to the definition."
        ],
        "links": [
          {
            "title": "Read the complete Memorabilia — English translation",
            "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1177/1177-h/1177-h.htm"
          },
          {
            "title": "Wikipedia: Memorabilia",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memorabilia_(Xenophon)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A definition under pressure",
        "body": [
          "Hippias challenges Socrates to state what justice is rather than only question other people. Socrates points to conduct and then connects the just with the lawful. Hippias’ objection that laws change is an important part of the conversation, not an interruption to be skipped on the way to a slogan.",
          "Distinguish three claims: that a city needs law, that citizens should obey law, and that every existing law is morally best. They are not interchangeable. Read Socrates’ reasons and Hippias’ objections before deciding how far the argument succeeds."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Unwritten laws",
        "body": [
          "The dialogue extends beyond written civic rules to obligations described as unwritten, including honoring parents and returning benefits. Socrates relates these to divine authority. Their universality is a claim made in the discussion, not a conclusion independently established about every human society.",
          "Compare an inscription, a customary expectation, and a claim about divine law. They have different kinds of authority and different ways of being challenged. The banner’s inscribed surface is a reconstruction without a fabricated readable ancient law; use the actual literary passage for the words being interpreted."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Return to Socrates’ refusal",
        "body": [
          "Revisit Lesson 40. Socrates’ resistance to a collective vote appeals to lawful procedure against immediate pressure. How does that example bear on the claim that lawful conduct is just? Explain a possible connection without pretending the two passages settle every problem about unjust laws.",
          "Translate the dialogue’s objection before its response. Then distinguish the course’s optative wishes from its potential statements. Write a wish for fair judgment and a possibility about what a citizen might do, and explain why neither is evidence that the action actually occurred."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "What does Hippias’ changing-law objection challenge?",
        "answer": "The connection between law and justice."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is the status of unwritten laws’ universality here?",
        "answer": "A claim made in the dialogue."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which distinction is essential?",
        "answer": "Needing law, obeying law, and calling every law best are different claims."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How does Lesson 40 help this discussion?",
        "answer": "It offers a concrete case of law against immediate political pressure."
      }
    ],
