        "body": [
          "In secondary sequence, an original finite indicative may appear as an optative: ἔλεγεν ὅτι τοῦτο δίκαιον εἴη. This reports what someone said, without turning the narrator into the original claimant. Original tense relations are preserved: a prior action is not made simultaneous merely because the main verb is past.",
          "The original mood can be retained: ἔλεγεν ὅτι τοῦτο δίκαιόν ἐστιν. Mixed reporting is possible, so annotate each clause rather than enforcing a mechanical rule. An accusative-and-infinitive report remains an infinitive construction; it is not converted to an optative solely because φησί becomes ἔφη."
