        "1. Present optatives of -μι verbs: The present optative retains the present stem: διδοίην, τιθείην, ἱσταίην, ἱείην. These forms are not aorists merely because they contain -ιη-. Learn them beside δίδωμι, τίθημι, ἵστημι, and ἵημι, and keep the reduplicated present stem visible.",
        "2. Aorist optatives and voice: The aorist active forms lose the present reduplication: δοίην, θείην, σταίην, εἵην. With δίδωμι and τίθημι, compare plural δοῖμεν and θεῖμεν; with intransitive ἔστην, σταῖμεν. The aorist of ἵημι has the rough breathing in εἵην, and compounds such as ἀφείην are common.",
        "3. Historical reporting and retained mood: In secondary sequence, an original finite indicative may appear as an optative: ἔλεγεν ὅτι τοῦτο δίκαιον εἴη. This reports what someone said, without turning the narrator into the original claimant. Original tense relations are preserved: a prior action is not made simultaneous merely because the main verb is past.",
        "4. Indirect questions and reconstructing direct speech: After a historical verb of asking or considering, a direct indicative or deliberative subjunctive may become optative: τί δῶ; becomes ἠρώτα τί δοίη, “He asked what he should give.” A yes/no question still needs εἰ, “whether.” Keep the question’s focus, not just the verb ending."
