    "translation": "After the battles, those in Piraeus and those in the city deliberate about peace. It is not enough to lay down arms; they must also find a way to live with one another. The victors remember their friends who died, while the others fear punishment. The city has one territory but divided opinions.\n\nAn agreement is therefore made, and each party returns to its own property. The terms distinguish the Thirty, the Eleven, and the ten who had ruled in Piraeus. The agreement does not include them in the same way. Those who were afraid were permitted to depart to Eleusis. Let us not omit this distinction when speaking about peace.\n\nThose from Piraeus go up to the Acropolis and sacrifice to Athena. The common constitution is then restored. Later new events occur concerning Eleusis, and not everything is accomplished without violence. Finally they swear not to recall past wrongs against one another. Xenophon says that the citizens abide by their oaths in the period about which he writes.\n\nNow in the lesson we compare three proposals. If we abide by our oaths, we shall be more trustworthy. If we were to abide by our oaths, we would be more trustworthy. If we had abided by our oaths, we would have been more trustworthy. The first expects a more definite future, the second presents a possibility more tentatively, and the third speaks counterfactually about a past event.\n\nWe consider not only possibilities but also necessities. We must keep the oaths; we must examine accusations, not punish people without judgment. Since it is necessary to live together, it is not useful constantly to increase old hatred. Let one be strong in body and another trustworthy in judgment. Not every kind of strength provides the same benefit.\n\nThe argument for peace does not command us to regard every wrong as good. It asks how those who disagree will inhabit the same city. If we provide safety only for friends, what will the others do? If we consider everyone suspect, how do we undertake common tasks? In asking these questions we write our own proposals, while separately examining what happened in the narrative.\n",
