      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Conditions: open and future more vivid: A conditional sentence has a protasis, the if-clause, and an apodosis, its consequence. An open condition uses εἰ with indicative and presents an assumption without marking it as contrary to fact: εἰ τοῦτο ποιεῖ, ἀδικεῖ. The tense can be present or past as the context requires.",
        "2. Future less vivid and general conditions: Future less vivid commonly uses εἰ with optative and an apodosis with optative plus ἄν: εἰ ἐμμένοιμεν, πιστοὶ ἂν εἴημεν. “If we were to abide, we would be trustworthy” presents a more tentative future possibility. It does not by itself say that we failed in the past.",
        "3. Contrary-to-fact conditions: A contrary-to-fact condition normally has εἰ with a past indicative, and a past indicative with ἄν in the consequence. The imperfect often marks a present unreal situation: εἰ δίκαιος ἦν, οὐκ ἂν ἠδίκει, “If he were just, he would not be doing wrong.” The aorist commonly marks a past unreal action.",
        "4. Adverbial accusative and respect: An accusative can measure extent in space or time without being a direct object: πολὺν χρόνον ἔμειναν, “They stayed a long time.” Other accusatives function adverbially. Identify the verb’s ordinary argument pattern before calling every accusative an object.",
        "5. Accusative absolute: An absolute construction supplies a circumstance without being a normal argument of the main clause. Impersonal participles often form an accusative absolute: ἐξόν, “it being permitted”; δέον, “it being necessary”; δυνατόν/ἀδύνατον ὄν, “it being possible/impossible.” An infinitive can complete the expression.",
        "6. Necessity with -τέος: Verbal adjectives in -τέος express what must be done. In a personal construction the adjective agrees with the thing requiring action: ὁ νόμος φυλακτέος ἐστίν, “The law must be kept.” A dative can identify the responsible agent: ἡμῖν, “by us.”"
