        "title": "2. Reduplication, vowels, and consonant stems",
        "practiceTopic": "reduplication",
        "body": [
          "A simple consonant often reduplicates with ε: λύω → λέλυμαι. Aspirated consonants use the corresponding unaspirated sound in reduplication: θύω → τέθυμαι, not an initial θ twice. Initial vowels commonly lengthen: ἀδικέω → ἠδίκημαι. Clusters and irregular verbs require principal parts; do not generate every perfect by repeating the first written letter.",
          "Consonant stems change at the junction with endings: γράφω gives γέγραμμαι, γέγραψαι, γέγραπται; πράττω gives πέπραγμαι, πέπραξαι, πέπρακται. Dental stems often yield σ before the ending, as πεπείσμαι. Consonant-stem third plurals frequently use a participle with εἰσί: γεγραμμένοι εἰσί."
