    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Perfect middle/passive: formation and state: The perfect commonly presents a resulting state: λέλυται can mean “has been released” and hence “is free.” The aorist ἐλύθη narrates the release as a whole. The perfect is not simply a more distant past tense. In the middle/passive, endings attach directly to the perfect stem, without a thematic vowel.",
        "2. Reduplication, vowels, and consonant stems: A simple consonant often reduplicates with ε: λύω → λέλυμαι. Aspirated consonants use the corresponding unaspirated sound in reduplication: θύω → τέθυμαι, not an initial θ twice. Initial vowels commonly lengthen: ἀδικέω → ἠδίκημαι. Clusters and irregular verbs require principal parts; do not generate every perfect by repeating the first written letter.",
        "3. Perfect participles and infinitives: The perfect middle/passive participle uses -μένος, -μένη, -μένον, normally accented on the syllable before the ending: λελυμένος. It agrees in gender, number, and case with its noun. ὁ λελυμένος, with an article, can mean “the released man”; without an article it can supply a circumstance or predicate.",
        "4. Pluperfect middle/passive: The pluperfect places the resulting state at a past reference point: ἐλέλυτο, “he had been released.” It normally adds an augment before the reduplicated stem and secondary middle/passive endings. Learn ἐλελύμην, ἐλέλυσο, ἐλέλυτο, ἐλελύμεθα, ἐλέλυσθε, ἐλέλυντο.",
        "5. Dative of agent and other datives: With perfect passive forms, a dative can mark the agent: ταῦτα ἡμῖν πέπρακται, “These things have been done by us.” It is particularly natural with a resulting state viewed as someone’s completed work. Ordinary passive agents can also be expressed with ὑπό plus genitive; a dative is not the universal agent construction in every tense."
      ]
