    "translation": "Socrates makes lofty claims about his life in Xenophon’s Apology. Not all the listeners are pleased: some make an uproar. The writer asks why the man has spoken this way. It is not enough to say that the jurors were bad. We must examine separately what was said, the speaker’s intention, and the listeners’ response.\n\nSocrates puts forward his own freedom and moderation. He says that he has mastered his desires and has not taken money from his associates. The listeners do not all receive what he says in the same way. One considers it evidence of virtue; another criticizes the lofty speech. The same utterance can provoke different judgments.\n\nIn another work, the Memorabilia, Xenophon writes about Critias and Alcibiades. These two associated with Socrates and later did much harm to the city. The accuser makes their association an accusation; Xenophon defends Socrates and examines how they used their education. We are not reading the same argument in the two works.\n\nBefore the trial the city had suffered much. War, defeat, and civil conflict had harmed the citizens. Some had lost friends; others went into exile from their homeland. Remembering these things is not the same as knowing what each juror thought. The past illuminates the trial, but it is not testimony about every opinion.\n\nWe therefore ask what we know and what we suppose. I know that Xenophon has written this; I do not know that every juror became angry for the same reason. We know what is written in the book, but must seek evidence concerning the event. Oida has perfect form but expresses present knowledge. Ēidein expresses knowledge at a past time.\n\nLet us compare three statements. The accuser spoke; the accuser has spoken; the accuser had already spoken before the other replied. The first narrates the act, the second connects the utterance with the present, and the third with a past reference time. Not every such difference appears in a single English word.\n\nJustice in judgment also demands precision in statements. “The man associated with them” is one thing; “The man has taught all their actions” another. The second needs more evidence. In this way we listen both to Socrates and to his opponents, putting neither friendship in place of evidence nor anger in place of argument.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Apology 20–23; contextual comparison with Memorabilia 1.2.9–12. Independently composed course adaptation, not a verbatim quotation. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1171/1171-h/1171-h.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "Based on Apology 20–23 with a separately labeled comparison to Memorabilia 1.2.9–12. Associations with Critias and Alcibiades are context, not substitute formal charges or proof of individual jurors’ motives. Reporting and perfect-system examples are course compositions."
  },
