        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Apology",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon’s Apology of Socrates is a short defense of the philosopher’s conduct at his trial in Athens in 399 BCE. “Apology” here means a defense, not an expression of regret. The work opens by asking why Socrates spoke with such elevated self-confidence. Xenophon wants the reader to understand the intention behind that manner, including Socrates’ judgment about his age, life, and approaching death. The choice of opening question shapes everything that follows.",
          "The work moves from a conversation with Hermogenes before the trial to selected courtroom exchanges, reactions, the verdict, and Socrates’ response. It does not attempt to reproduce every procedural stage or every word spoken. Xenophon explicitly names Hermogenes as an informant in section 2. That is valuable evidence about a chain of testimony, but it does not turn the finished literary work into a transcript. Ask when the narrator summarizes, when Socrates answers an objection, and when a friend’s report supplies the connection.",
          "Plato wrote a different Apology, and the two works should be read as distinct presentations. Do not silently import Plato’s famous phrases or penalty proposals into Xenophon’s account. Xenophon also defends Socrates across the four books of the Memorabilia, where conversations and habits provide a broader portrait. Read sections 1–9 for the preparation, 10–26 for the selected trial account, and the closing sections for the remembered aftermath. The complete translation linked here makes it possible to see the lesson’s excerpt in its argumentative setting. Across Lessons 45–47, notice how preparation, reputation, and courtroom reporting each illuminate a different part of Xenophon’s purpose."
        ],
        "links": [
          {
            "title": "Read the complete Apology — English translation",
            "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1171/1171-h/1171-h.htm"
          },
          {
            "title": "Wikipedia: Apology",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apology_(Xenophon)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Claims that provoke reactions",
        "body": [
          "In Xenophon’s Apology, Socrates’ elevated claims provoke noisy reactions. The author explains the manner as part of Socrates’ deliberate approach to his situation. An explanation of a speaker’s intention and a report of an audience’s reaction answer different questions.",
          "Avoid drawing every juror as an irrational member of a single mob. We do not have access to each juror’s inner reasoning. The reading asks you to examine the speech and the narrator’s interpretation while acknowledging what the evidence does not reveal."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Association is not an indictment",
        "body": [
          "Memorabilia 1.2 discusses Critias and Alcibiades and their association with Socrates. That passage supplies a separate strand in Xenophon’s defense of his teacher. It must not be inserted into the Apology as if it were an additional formal charge preserved there.",
          "Timeline: Arginusae was in 406 BCE; the Thirty and democratic restoration belong to 404–403; Socrates’ trial was in 399. Recent violence and political trauma are relevant context. They do not establish a single motive shared by every person who voted against him."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Sort the claims",
        "body": [
          "Create four headings: formal charge, association, narrator’s explanation, and conjecture. Place each statement you make about hostility toward Socrates under one heading and supply its source. If a claim fits more than one category, explain which part is documented and which is inferred.",
          "Translate the pluperfect account of the city’s recent suffering, then the distinctions involving οἶδα. Finish with the last two paragraphs as a connected translation. Revise one overconfident claim by replacing certainty with the exact degree of support the source provides."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Which sequence is chronological?",
        "answer": "Arginusae, Thirty/restoration, Socrates’ trial."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What does association with Critias and Alcibiades establish by itself?",
        "answer": "An association requiring analysis, not proof Socrates taught their crimes."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Where does that comparison appear in the lesson’s sources?",
        "answer": "Memorabilia, separately from Apology."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How should claims about individual juror motives be handled?",
        "answer": "With uncertainty unless evidence supports them."
      }
    ],
