    "translation": "Xenophon narrates Socrates’ defense and sets out the accusations. The accuser says that Socrates does not acknowledge the city’s gods, introduces new divine things, and corrupts the young. These are accusations, not matters already accepted by the reader. Let us therefore first hear precisely what each person says, and then how Socrates replies.\n\nSocrates replies that he sacrifices to the gods, including at public sanctuaries. He says that many have seen him sacrificing. He does not merely say “I acknowledge gods,” but supplies deeds as witnesses. Concerning the divine sign, he draws a comparison with divination. Other people too seek signs from the gods through voices and birds.\n\nThe accuser says that he introduces novelties, while Socrates compares his own practice with that of others. Here the reader must distinguish two voices. Xenophon writes that Socrates says that the gods give him signs. The writer transmits the account; Socrates makes the response; the gods are the subject of the action within that response.\n\nConcerning the young, Socrates questions Meletus. He says that it must be made clear whom he has turned from good to bad. The harm, not simply the association, must be demonstrated. Meletus says that the young obey Socrates more than their parents. Socrates therefore responds about education and expertise; he does not pass over the question in silence.\n\nWhenever we investigate health, we obey the doctor; concerning a journey, we follow someone who knows the road. We do not thereby dishonor our parents. Socrates asks that education be judged in this way. The reader examines whether the example resolves every question concerning the young. We must report the argument faithfully even when we do not concede everything to it.\n\nNot all the jurors are persuaded, and Socrates is convicted. Xenophon relates that Socrates is unwilling to propose a counterpenalty in place of death: he considers this an admission of wrongdoing. We are reading Xenophon’s account here. Other works transmit other speeches, and we should not join them into a single defense without indicating the change.\n\nThe friends grieve, but Socrates maintains his own judgment. The writer depicts him as preferring not merely living but living well. When we report the argument, let us not leave the listener unaware of who is speaking. The accuser alleges, Socrates replies, Xenophon explains, and the reader judges. Here both grammar and justice demand precision.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Apology 10–26, selected connected passages. Independently composed course adaptation, not a verbatim quotation. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1171/1171-h/1171-h.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "A connected teaching adaptation of Xenophon’s Apology 10–26. Present reporting frames and transitions are composed for instruction. Plato’s courtroom speeches and proposed penalties are not inserted. The death scene belongs to the separate Culture/History discussion of Plato’s Phaedo."
  },
