    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Mapping a sustained primary-sequence report: Begin with the governing verb: λέγει, φησί, γράφει, or a future reporting verb. Mark the boundary of each report before translating. In ὁ Ξενοφῶν γράφει ὅτι ὁ Σωκράτης φησὶ τοὺς θεοὺς σημαίνειν, there are three levels: Xenophon writes; Socrates says; the gods give signs. Each verb has its own subject.",
        "2. Relative time in nonfinite reports: Under a reporting verb, distinguish simultaneous, prior, subsequent, and perfect-result relations. φησὶ θύειν, θῦσαι, θύσειν, τεθυκέναι can report “is sacrificing,” “sacrificed,” “will sacrifice,” and “has sacrificed.” The future infinitive retains its future relation even if the report is made now.",
        "3. Pronouns and the scope of negation: A report can change the person of pronouns while retaining the identity of their referents. Direct “I acknowledge gods” may become “Socrates says that he acknowledges gods.” If several men are present, English “he” may be less clear than a repeated name. Greek reflexives can point back to the reporting subject; ordinary pronouns require contextual resolution.",
        "4. Subordinate clauses inside reports: A report may contain a purpose, condition, relative, or question. Bracket it before translating: λέγει ὅτι, ἐὰν οἱ νέοι μανθάνωσιν, βελτίους ἔσονται. The condition belongs to the reported claim, while the outer ὅτι introduces that claim. Its subjunctive is licensed by ἐάν, not by the outer verb λέγει."
      ]
    }
