          },
          {
            "title": "Wikipedia: Phaedo",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaedo"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "How Xenophon might have learned",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon was away from Athens and did not witness the execution. In Apology 2 and Memorabilia 4.8.4 he explicitly identifies Hermogenes as an informant about Socrates’ attitude and preparation. Plato also lists Hermogenes among the companions present on the final day at Phaedo 59b. This gives us a documented connection to someone represented as close to the events.",
          "We do not know who first brought Xenophon news of the death, precisely where he received it, or when that first report reached him. Travelers, fellow soldiers, letters, and conversations with friends are plausible channels, but no particular messenger, letter, or scene should be narrated as established fact. Distinguish the attested informant from the uncertain first arrival of the news."
