    "translation": "At the end of the Memorabilia Xenophon examines Socrates’ life. He tells not only of his death, but how the man lived and benefited his companions. He wrote that Socrates was pious and just and had become master of pleasures. These are words of praise; the reader must also recall the evidence for the judgment.\n\nHermogenes told him about the conversation before the trial. He said that Socrates was not unaware of the danger, but regarded his life as a sufficient defense. Hermogenes asked how this could be and whether he was taking the jurors into account. Socrates replied that he had already considered these matters. The writer received his friend’s account.\n\nIn reporting, it is not necessary to change every verb. The writer said that Socrates is just; the writer said that Socrates was just, with an optative. Both reports are possible, but differ in form. “He said that he was just,” using an infinitive, does not change in the same way. The infinitive indicates time simultaneous with the reporting.\n\nXenophon said that Socrates had benefited his friends. The benefit occurred before the report, and the deed still matters for the judgment. He wrote that Socrates appeared confident after the verdict. Here we must consider who benefited others, who wrote, and to what time each statement refers. A careful reader does not merely count endings, but connects actions.\n\nFrom this we turn to another work of Xenophon. In the Hellenica he wrote about the Athenians’ reconciliation. He said that the citizens abided by their oaths. He did not say that no one had previously done wrong, but that they were trying to live together. Thus, in judging both a life and a city, we ask which deeds confirm trust and which statements merely look attractive.\n\nCyrus confused usefulness with justice; household owners sought careful management; soldiers judged their general. In the city law offered protection, but fear removed it. These are not a single history, but many examples from different works. Each must be related to its setting, time, and speaker.\n\nWhat, then, do we say about the soul? Here we examine character and choice; we do not add arguments about immortality to Xenophon. Character is examined through actions: how do we use power, whom do we hear, and what explanations do we accept? If we praised only our friends, we would not judge justly. If we examine the evidence, we shall become better judges.\n\nFinally the learner writes a judgment of their own. Let them choose two examples and make the evidence clear. One need not concede everything to Xenophon, but must report faithfully what he says. Thus the account becomes an exercise not only in language but in judgment. The course ends, but the inquiry into justice remains.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Memorabilia 4.8.1–11; brief return to Hellenica 2.4.43. Independently composed course adaptation, not a verbatim quotation. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1177/1177-h/1177-h.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "A final connected course adaptation drawing on Memorabilia 4.8.1–11 and, in a separately marked comparison, Hellenica 2.4.43. The closing synthesis and questions are course compositions. “Soul” concerns character and ethical life here, not an attribution to Xenophon of Plato’s immortality arguments."
  },
