          "Read a sustained passage twice. First identify the argument, work, speaker, and chronology. Then bracket its clauses and annotate unfamiliar forms. Use subjunctive and optative signals, conditional systems, articular infinitives, absolute constructions, verbal adjectives, and perfect aspect to explain how the argument is built. Glossed exposure is support, not a demand to reproduce untaught paradigms.",
          "For the final translation, write connected English that preserves attribution and distinctions in time. Recheck pronouns, negation, and argument structure before polishing style. In the accompanying reflection cite two course episodes and distinguish what the author records from what you infer. A reasoned disagreement with Xenophon can earn full credit when the Greek and evidence are represented accurately."
