    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Secondary sequence in connected reporting: Historical reporting frames such as ἔλεγεν and ἔγραφεν can introduce an oblique optative in place of an original finite indicative or subjunctive. Present δίκαιός ἐστι becomes δίκαιος εἴη; an original deliberative τί ποιήσω; can become ἠρώτα τί ποιήσειε. The shift places the claim within a past report without erasing its original temporal relation.",
        "2. Infinitives and participles retain relative time: An infinitive report remains nonfinite after a historical reporting verb: ἔφη τὸν Σωκράτη ὠφελεῖν/ὠφελῆσαι/ὠφελήσειν. These are simultaneous, prior, and subsequent relative to the past report. ὠφεληκέναι presents a completed action with its relevant result. None must become an optative merely because ἔφη is past.",
        "3. Nested speakers, questions, and negation: In ἔγραφεν ὅτι ὁ Ἑρμογένης ἔλεγεν ὅτι ὁ Σωκράτης δίκαιος εἴη, distinguish writer, informant, and subject of the judgment. A pronoun can otherwise seem to jump between them. Use repeated names in your translation when needed, while preserving the source’s attribution.",
        "4. Cumulative translation: form, argument, and evidence: Read a sustained passage twice. First identify the argument, work, speaker, and chronology. Then bracket its clauses and annotate unfamiliar forms. Use subjunctive and optative signals, conditional systems, articular infinitives, absolute constructions, verbal adjectives, and perfect aspect to explain how the argument is built. Glossed exposure is support, not a demand to reproduce untaught paradigms."
      ]
    }
